commit 5c0d4ec9ab9c96974c58b73a3e9fa87bc385b2dc
Author: Miller Lai <miller.lai@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Aug 31 06:07:23 2026 -0400

    fix(cai): decode gh's output as UTF-8, and summarise what gets logged
    
    Two bugs the end-to-end run found against a real issue. Both were present
    while all 186 unit tests were green, because the fakes those tests drive
    only ever emit ASCII and only ever check the helper, not the call.
    
    **Decoding.** run() copied preflight.py's `subprocess.run(text=True)` shape,
    which decodes using the console locale. That is fine for git, whose output is
    essentially ASCII; gh echoes back comment bodies, issue titles and this
    project's own state.md notes. The first projection succeeded and the second
    died reading back the Chinese comment the first one had just written --
    UnicodeDecodeError, raised inside subprocess's own reader thread, where the
    function's `except` clause cannot see it. It never propagated; it just left
    stdout as None, so json.loads(None) was the first visible symptom, three
    frames away from the cause. encoding="utf-8" is now explicit, and every JSON
    parse goes through a guard that returns a category instead of raising, so an
    undecodable byte can no longer make a Backend method raise.
    
    **Logging.** The design asks for `backend <argv summary> -> <category>`, and
    the summary part is load-bearing: the body handed to `-f body=...` is a whole
    six-row table, so logging argv verbatim printed the entire mirrored comment on
    every projection. Arguments are now flattened and clipped.
    
    The second fix was itself wrong on the first attempt, in a way worth recording:
    only one of run()'s two print sites was changed, and the tests written for it
    exercised the summary helper rather than run(), so they passed while the line a
    projection actually prints was unchanged. The test added here drives run() and
    asserts on its real output; reverting either print site now fails it.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

